-0.16 0.17c -0.05 0 -0.1 -0.04 -0.16 -0.12c -0.33 -0.44 -0.74 -0.72 -1.21 -0.84c -0.31 -0.08 -0.54 -0.12 -0.71 -0.12c -0.57 0 -0.85 0.35 -0.85 1.04c 0 0.62 0.29 1.52 0.53 1.95c 0.24 0.44 0.75 0.96 1.34 0.96c 0.38 0 0.64 -0.11 0.78 -0.32c -0.31 -0.03 -0.58 -0.23 -0.58 -0.56Z \"></path></symbol><symbol id=\"heliae7e47287-gAE9FF52A1D42223AC1C1C189F82D6586\" overflow=\"visible\"><path d=\"M 0 0m 4.29 6.32l -0.59 -2.43c -0.21 0.37 -0.5 0.56 -0.89 0.56c -0.65 0 -1.22 -0.33 -1.72 -1c -0.46 -0.62 -0.69 -1.27 -0.69 -1.94c 0 -0.88 0.51 -1.62 1.35 -1.62c 0.43 0 0.85 0.23 1.25 0.7c 0.11 -0.38 0.45 -0.7 0.92 -0.7c 0.69 0 0.91 0.82 1.06 1.56c 0 0.09 -0.05 0.14 -0.15 0.14c -0.09 0 -0.15 -0.07 -0.18 -0.21c -0.2 -0.79 -0.44 -1.19 -0.71 -1.19c -0.17 0 -0.26 0.14 -0.26 0.41c 0 0.15 0.02 0.31 0.06 0.47l 1.42 5.72v 0.04c -0.03 0.07 -0.09 0.11 -0.17 0.11c -0.23 0 -0.65 -0.04 -1.25 -0.11c -0.11 -0.01 -0.17 -0.09 -0.17 -0.23c 0 -0.1 0.09 -0.15 0.27 -0.15c 0.19 0 0.45 0.01 0.45 -0.13Z m -0.88 -2.56c 0.1 -0.21 0.15 -0.35 0.15 -0.44c -0.01 -0.04 -0.02 -0.09 -0.03 -0.16l -0.49 -1.94c -0.03 -0.11 -0.1 -0.23 -0.19 -0.35c -0.37 -0.45 -0.73 -0.68 -1.08 -0.68c -0.39 0 -0.59 0.3 -0.59 0.89c 0 0.24 0.06 0.6 0.18 1.08c 0.21 0.85 0.51 1.43 0.88 1.74c 0.2 0.17 0.39 0.25 0.58 0.25c 0.27 0 0.47 -0.13 0.59 -0.39Z \"></path></symbol></defs></svg>"]],"body":{"Block":[]}}},{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"We say that "},{"Math":{"src":"p"}},{"Text":" satisfies the universal property of the product of "},{"Math":{"src":"c"}},{"Text":" and "},{"Math":{"src":"d"}},{"Text":"."}]},{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"Surely this matches our set-based intuition of what a product should behave like. Similarly, we can sketch out constructions of other universal properties like initial objects, terminal objects, exponentials, etc. However, what is precisely meant by the term "},{"Emph":[{"Text":"universal property"}]},{"Text":"?"}]},{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"The notion of a universal property is made precise by the notion of a "},{"EntryLink":{"id":"universal-element","label":null,"body":[{"Text":"universal element of a presheaf"}]}},{"Text":". That is, an object satisfies a universal property if we can build a universal element of the appropriate presheaf at that object."}]},{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"Let’s look at the universal element characterization of the products example. Note that a map into a product is determined by a map into each component. To map into "},{"Math":{"src":"c × d"}},{"Text":", we need both a map into "},{"Math":{"src":"c"}},{"Text":" and a map into "},{"Math":{"src":"d"}},{"Text":", as in the above diagram. That is, to build a map "},{"Math":{"src":"b -> c × d"}},{"Text":", we must simultaneously provide elements of "},{"Math":{"src":"よ c"}},{"Text":" and "},{"Math":{"src":"よ d"}},{"Text":" at "},{"Math":{"src":"b"}},{"Text":"."}]},{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"Using the product of presheaves, this means we are providing a single element of the presheaf "},{"Math":{"src":"( よ c ) × ( よ d )"}},{"Text":". Quite nicely, the universal element of this presheaf provides the object of "},{"Math":{"src":"C"}},{"Text":" that is "},{"Emph":[{"Text":"the product"}]},{"Text":" of "},{"Math":{"src":"c"}},{"Text":" and "},{"Math":{"src":"d"}},{"Text":". The universal element, provided that it exists, contains the following data:"}]},{"List":{"marker":null,"tight":true,"items":[{"Inline":[{"Text":"An object "},{"Math":{"src":"p"}}]},{"Inline":[{"Text":"An element "},{"Math":{"src":"x ∈ ( よ c × よ d ) p"}}]},{"Inline":[{"Text":"A proof that the map sending "},{"Math":{"src":"f : b -> p"}},{"Text":" to a pair of maps "},{"Math":{"src":"f_1 : b -> c"}},{"Text":", "},{"Math":{"src":"f_2 : b -> d"}},{"Text":" is an equivalence. Therefore, any element of "},{"Math":{"src":"よ c × よ d"}},{"Text":" factors through "},{"Math":{"src":"x"}}]}]}},{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"Recall that the product of presheaves is computed pointwise in the category of sets, so if we expand the type of the element "},{"Math":{"src":"x"}},{"Text":" above we find that "},{"Math":{"src":"x"}},{"Text":" is a pair of maps "},{"Math":{"src":"p -> c"}},{"Text":" and "},{"Math":{"src":"p -> d"}},{"Text":"."}]},{"Math":{"src":"( よ c × よ d ) p ≅ ( よ c p ) × ( よ d p )"}},{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"The first part of this pair is precisely "},{"Math":{"src":"pi_1"}},{"Text":". Correspondingly, the second part of this pair is "},{"Math":{"src":"pi_2"}},{"Text":". Finally, the universality of the element "},{"Math":{"src":"x"}},{"Text":" (i.e. the proof that any other element factors through "},{"Math":{"src":"x"}},{"Text":") captures our commutative diagram from above."}]},{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"This is a very rough sketch of what a universal property is, and has elided for now an important application of the Yoneda lemma. In any case, all a universal property is really saying is that a particular presheaf is representable; and, rather elegantly, a universal element of a presheaf is convenient packaging of that representability proof."}]},{"Paragraph":[{"Text":"In summary, "},{"Emph":[{"Text":"universal properties"}]},{"Text":" are not as ad-hoc as they may initially seem, and the language of presheaves provides a reusable and precise definition that can be instantiated to describe a very large class of properties."}]}]}}}]}}
